Getting Started With POCO OSP
Open Service Platform

!!!Building POCO OSP

POCO OSP is built with CMake. The configure pulls the POCO C++ Libraries, and
the build compiles the POCO libraries that the enabled OSP parts need together
with POCO OSP.

!!Prerequisites

  - CMake 3.26 or newer. Ubuntu 22.04 ships 3.22 and Debian 12 ships 3.25; a
    newer one is in [[https://apt.kitware.com Kitware's APT repository]]
    (Ubuntu) or in bookworm-backports (Debian 12), or can be installed with
    <[pipx install cmake]>.
  - A C++20 compiler; the configure stops without one.
  - git, with which the configure pulls POCO.
  - OpenSSL 1.1.1 or newer, which the Crypto and NetSSL libraries of POCO need.
    The parts that use them are enabled by default.

Optional libraries, each needed by one option (see below):

  - libssh: <[ENABLE_OSP_SHELL_SSH]>.
  - OpenLDAP: <[ENABLE_OSP_AUTH_LDAP]>. On Windows, the system LDAP library
    is used; macOS is not supported.
  - unixODBC: <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_ODBC]>.
  - The MySQL or MariaDB client library: <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_MYSQL]>.
  - The PostgreSQL client library: <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_POSTGRESQL]>.
  - libedit, for line editing in the shell client: <[ENABLE_OSP_SHELL_CLIENT]>.
    Without it, the shell client reads plain lines.

On Debian and Ubuntu, the required packages are installed with

    $ sudo apt-get install cmake git g++ libssl-dev
----

On macOS, with Homebrew:

    $ brew install cmake openssl@3
----

Homebrew does not install OpenSSL where CMake looks for it, so the configure
on macOS gets <[-DOPENSSL_ROOT_DIR=$(brew --prefix openssl@3)]>.

!!Configuring and Building

In the source directory of POCO OSP:

    $ cmake -S . -B cmake-build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    $ cmake --build cmake-build --parallel
----

The libraries and tools are written to <[cmake-build/lib]> and
<[cmake-build/bin]>, and the bundles to <[cmake-build/bundles]>.
At the end of the configure, a summary names the POCO that is built, the
enabled options, and the options that are off, with the reason where the
configure has switched one off.

!!Selecting POCO

Two cache variables select the POCO that the configure pulls:

  - <[POCO_OSP_POCO_REPOSITORY]>: a git URL, or a local directory, relative
    to the source directory of POCO OSP. The default is
    <[https://github.com/pocoproject/poco.git]>. A local git repository is
    cloned like a remote one, so it works offline. A directory that is a POCO
    source tree without <[.git]> is used as it is, and <[POCO_OSP_POCO_VERSION]>
    does not apply.
  - <[POCO_OSP_POCO_VERSION]>: <[release]> (the default) is the newest POCO
    2.x release, or POCO <[main]> while no such release exists;
    <[latest]> is POCO <[main]>; any other value is a tag, a branch or a commit
    of the repository.

The configure resolves <[release]> and <[latest]> to a tag or a commit once, and
a reconfigure builds the same POCO. To resolve them again:

    $ cmake -U POCO_OSP_POCO_RESOLVED_REF cmake-build
----

A POCO source tree can also be given with <[FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_POCO]>, as
an absolute path; it is used as it is.

POCO OSP needs POCO 2.0 or newer. A POCO without the RemotingNG library, which
POCO has from version 2.0 on, stops the configure.

A project that adds the POCO sources itself (with <[add_subdirectory()]> or
<[FetchContent]>) and then adds POCO OSP with <[add_subdirectory()]> builds
POCO OSP with that POCO, and nothing is pulled. The project then enables the
POCO components that the OSP options need; a missing one stops the configure
with a message that names the POCO option. An installed POCO cannot be used.

The project also owns the install RPATH of the POCO libraries. When the
project does not set <[CMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH]>, POCO OSP sets it in its own
directory only, for its own targets; the POCO libraries that the project adds
get none. If the installed tools must run from a prefix outside the search path
of the dynamic loader, the project sets <[CMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH]> before it adds
POCO, on Linux for example:

    include(GNUInstallDirs)
    set(CMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH "$ORIGIN/../${CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR}")
    add_subdirectory(poco)
----

!!Options

The options select the parts of POCO OSP. A part whose POCO component the
pulled POCO does not build, for example SSH without libssh, is switched off
with a warning. An option that is ON while an option that it needs is OFF
stops the configure.

  - <[ENABLE_OSP]>: the OSP core library. Default ON.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_BUNDLE_CREATOR]>: the bundle creator tool <[bundle]>. Default ON.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_BUNDLES]>: creating bundles; needed by every bundle below.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_BUNDLESIGN]>: the bundle signing library and the tool <[signbundle]>.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_STRIPBUNDLE]>: the tool <[stripbundle]>.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_CODECACHE]>: the code cache utility <[ccutil]>.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_CORE]>: the Core bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_NET]>: the Net bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_NETSSL]>: the NetSSL_OpenSSL, SecureWebServer and SecureMail bundles.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_CRYPTO]>: the Crypto bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA]>, <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_SQLITE]>: the Data and Data SQLite bundles.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_ODBC]>, <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_MYSQL]>, <[ENABLE_OSP_DATA_POSTGRESQL]>:
    the Data ODBC, MySQL and PostgreSQL bundles. Default OFF.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_ACTIVERECORD]>: the ActiveRecord bundle. Default OFF.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_REDIS]>: the Redis bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_JWT]>: the JWT bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_MAIL]>: the Mail bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_PROMETHEUS]>: the Prometheus bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_WEB]>: the Web, WebServer and WebEvent bundles.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_BUNDLEADMIN]>: the BundleAdmin bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_AUTH]>: the Auth bundles.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_AUTH_LDAP]>: LDAP support in the Auth bundles. Default OFF.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_SIMPLEAUTH]>: the SimpleAuth bundle.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_SHELL]>: the Shell bundles.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_SHELL_SSH]>: the SSH server of the Shell. Default OFF.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_SHELL_CLIENT]>: the shell client <[ospsh]>. Default OFF.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_REMOTING]>: the RemotingNG TCP and REST bundles and the Auth Remoting library.
  - <[ENABLE_OSP_UNIT]>: the OSP Unit test runner bundle. Default OFF.
  - <[POCO_OSP_MINIMAL_BUILD]>: builds only the core library, the bundle
    creator and the parts enabled explicitly. Default OFF.
  - <[POCO_OSP_ENABLE_TESTS]>: the test suites. Default OFF; in a project that
    adds the POCO sources itself, the value of POCO's <[ENABLE_TESTS]>.
  - <[POCO_OSP_ENABLE_SAMPLES]>: the samples. Default OFF; in a project that
    adds the POCO sources itself, the value of POCO's <[ENABLE_SAMPLES]>.

The options without a default above are ON, unless <[POCO_OSP_MINIMAL_BUILD]>
is ON. With <[BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF]>, the configure switches off
<[ENABLE_OSP_BUNDLES]>, the parts that need it, and the tests, because
bundles are shared libraries.

!!Running the Tests

    $ cmake -S . -B cmake-build -DPOCO_OSP_ENABLE_TESTS=ON
    $ cmake --build cmake-build --parallel
    $ ctest --test-dir cmake-build --output-on-failure
----

The test suites of POCO itself are not built.


!!!Installing

    $ cmake --install cmake-build --prefix /opt/poco
----

This installs POCO and POCO OSP into the same prefix: the libraries, the
headers, the tools, the CMake package files in <[<libdir>/cmake/Poco]>, where
<[<libdir>]> is <[lib]> or <[lib64]> (in <[cmake]> on Windows), and the
bundles in <[bundles]>.

A CMake project finds the OSP libraries as components of the POCO package:

    find_package(Poco REQUIRED COMPONENTS OSP OSPWeb)
    target_link_libraries(MyApp PRIVATE Poco::OSP Poco::OSPWeb)
----

On Windows x86 and x64, an application that loads bundles using POCO Crypto or
NetSSL also links <[OpenSSL::applink]> from <[find_package(OpenSSL)]>: OpenSSL
ends a process whose executable does not provide <[OPENSSL_Applink]> when such
a bundle passes it a file opened by the C runtime.

The component <[OSPBundleCreator]> provides the bundle creator as the imported
target <[Poco::OSP::BundleCreator]>, and the CMake functions
<[poco_osp_bundle()]>, <[poco_osp_bundle_module()]> and <[poco_remote_gen()]>.
The CMake prefix path of the project names the installation:

    $ cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/poco
----


!!!A First Bundle

A bundle consists of a bundle specification, which the bundle creator
turns into the bundle manifest, and a shared library with the bundle
activator. The following project builds a bundle that logs a message when
it starts and when it stops.

<[Hello.bndlspec]>:

    <?xml version="1.0"?>
    <bundlespec>
      <manifest>
        <name>Hello Bundle</name>
        <symbolicName>com.example.hello</symbolicName>
        <version>1.0.0</version>
        <vendor>Example</vendor>
        <copyright>(c) 2026, Example</copyright>
        <activator>
          <class>HelloBundleActivator</class>
          <library>com.example.hello</library>
        </activator>
        <lazyStart>false</lazyStart>
        <runLevel>500</runLevel>
      </manifest>
    </bundlespec>
----

<[src/HelloBundleActivator.cpp]>:

    #include "Poco/OSP/BundleActivator.h"
    #include "Poco/OSP/BundleContext.h"
    #include "Poco/OSP/Bundle.h"
    #include "Poco/ClassLibrary.h"


    namespace
    {
        class HelloBundleActivator: public Poco::OSP::BundleActivator
        {
        public:
            void start(Poco::OSP::BundleContext::Ptr pContext) override
            {
                pContext->logger().information("Hello from " + pContext->thisBundle()->name() + ".");
            }

            void stop(Poco::OSP::BundleContext::Ptr pContext) override
            {
                pContext->logger().information("Goodbye from " + pContext->thisBundle()->name() + ".");
            }
        };
    }


    POCO_BEGIN_MANIFEST(Poco::OSP::BundleActivator)
        POCO_EXPORT_CLASS(HelloBundleActivator)
    POCO_END_MANIFEST
----

<[CMakeLists.txt]>:

    cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.26)
    project(Hello LANGUAGES CXX)

    find_package(Poco REQUIRED COMPONENTS OSP OSPBundleCreator)

    poco_osp_bundle_module(com.example.hello src/HelloBundleActivator.cpp)
    target_link_libraries(com.example.hello PRIVATE Poco::OSP)

    poco_osp_bundle(HelloBundle
        DEPENDS com.example.hello
        SPEC ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/Hello.bndlspec
        CODE $<TARGET_FILE:com.example.hello>
        DESTINATION ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/bundles
    )
----

<[poco_osp_bundle_module()]> adds the library with the file name that POCO OSP
loads, and <[poco_osp_bundle()]> runs the bundle creator, which writes
<[bundles/com.example.hello_1.0.0.bndl]>:

    $ cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/poco -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    $ cmake --build build
----

For a Debug build of the bundle, see [[00210-OSPBundleCreator.html The Bundle Creator Tool]].

The <*BundleContainer*> sample starts the bundles of its bundle repository,
stops them again and exits. It is not installed. In the source directory of
POCO OSP, it is built into <[cmake-build/bin]> with

    $ cmake -S . -B cmake-build -DPOCO_OSP_ENABLE_SAMPLES=ON
    $ cmake --build cmake-build --parallel
----

A configuration file, given with <[-f]>, makes the bundle directory of the
project the bundle repository. Its paths are relative to the current
directory:

<[hello.properties]>:

    osp.bundleRepository = build/bundles
    osp.codeCache        = build/codeCache
    osp.data             = build/data
----

In the directory of the project, with <[POCO_OSP]> set to the source directory
of POCO OSP:

    $ $POCO_OSP/cmake-build/bin/BundleContainer -f hello.properties
----

The log shows the messages of the bundle:

    osp.bundle.com.example.hello: [Information] Hello from Hello Bundle.
    osp.bundle.com.example.hello: [Information] Goodbye from Hello Bundle.
----

The [[00150-OSPTutorial.html OSP Tutorial]] continues with services and the
service registry.
